## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical Azure primitives for beginners: Functions HTTP trigger, Cosmos DB per-tenant partitioning, idempotency, TTL, Key Vault access via managed identity, and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Azure Functions HTTP trigger
- Cosmos DB partitioning by tenantId
- Idempotency with idempotencyKey
- TTL configuration
- Managed Identity and Key Vault secrets
- Basic Application Insights telemetry

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal outline showing Key Vault and Cosmos DB usage
const { DefaultAzureCredential } = require('@azure/identity');
const { SecretClient } = require('@azure/keyvault-secrets');
const { CosmosClient } = require('@azure/cosmos');

const credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();
const kv = new SecretClient(`https://MY_VAULT.vault.azure.net`, credential);

async function getCosmosConn() {
  const secret = await kv.getSecret('CosmosDBConnectionString');
  return secret.value;
}

async function storeEvent(tenantId, evt) {
  const conn = await getCosmosConn();
  const client = new CosmosClient(conn);
  const container = client.database('Telemetry').container('Events');
  await container.items.create({ ...evt, tenantId });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement idempotency key storage and cleanup?
- How would you test TTL expiration and ensure data isolation per-tenant?

Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a small, reliable serverless API with per-tenant isolation, idempotent writes, and TTL in Cosmos DB. It also checks basic validation, simple retries, and test strategy.

Key Concepts

- Azure Functions HTTP trigger and route parameters
- Cosmos DB partitioning by tenantId and idempotent upserts
- JSON payload validation against a minimal schema
- TTL retention in Cosmos DB containers
- Basic retry/backoff and error handling
- Unit and end-to-end testing with local emulation

Code Example

```javascript
// Example outline for the HTTP POST handler
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const tenantId = req.params.tenantId;
  const body = req.body;
  // basic validation: eventId required, payload present
  if (!tenantId || !body || !body.eventId) {
    context.res = { status: 400, body: 'Invalid payload' };
    return;
  }
  const item = {
    id: body.eventId,
    tenantId,
    payload: body,
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString()
  };
  // upsert to Cosmos DB (idempotent by using eventId as id)
  await cosmosClient.database(DATABASE).container(CONTAINER)
    .items.upsert(item);
  context.res = { status: 200, body: { ok: true } };
};
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this with a local Cosmos DB emulator and simulate retries?
- How would you evolve the schema when new fields are introduced while preserving backward compatibility?

